# Research Problem

For a tree T on n vertices, define

$$
q=\lceil\sqrt n\rceil
$$

and let n2(T) be the number of degree-2 vertices.

The target statement is

$$
n_2(T)\le2q-2
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
b(T)\le q.
$$

Its contrapositive is

$$
b(T)\ge q+1
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
n_2(T)\ge2q-1.
$$

We now want to understand the STRUCTURAL reason that the number

$$
2q-1
$$

might arise.

# Existing Exact Computations

The exact burning-number algorithm has already been validated.

Complete exhaustive computations are available for:

$$
5\le n\le19.
$$

In particular:

* all non-isomorphic trees for each n were generated;
* trees satisfying the relevant n2 boundary were exactly evaluated;
* no counterexample was found through n=19.

Do NOT rerun the full n<=19 exhaustive search unless necessary to recover missing data.

Use existing saved data whenever possible.

# Main Objective

Compare two populations:

## Population A — Boundary trees

For each n, let

$$
q=\lceil\sqrt n\rceil.
$$

Collect trees satisfying

$$
n_2(T)\le2q-2
$$

with the largest possible burning number, especially trees with

$$
b(T)=q.
$$

These are the trees that come closest to violating the conjecture while still satisfying the degree-2 boundary.

## Population B — Burning obstructions

Collect trees with

$$
b(T)\ge q+1,
$$

even if they have many more than \(2q-2\) degree-2 vertices.

These are the actual burning obstructions.

The purpose is to compare their structures.

